{
  "name": "malformed",
  "integration": "opennms-horizon",
  "description": "Every collection carries elements that the OpenNMS model classes say cannot occur, mixed in with rows that are merely typed inconsistently - which OpenNMS genuinely does. The node array holds a bare string and a bare number where OnmsNodeList declares a list of objects; one node has a null id; one has its assetRecord as the string \"unavailable\" where OnmsAssetRecord is an object; one has categories as a string where it is a list; one carries createTime as the ISO 8601 string that appears throughout the OpenNMS documentation, which is the XML representation and never the JSON one, where Jackson really emits epoch milliseconds as a bare integer; and one carries the node id as a JSON NUMBER rather than the string the v1 node record uses. The interface collections are equally hostile: an ipInterface whose nodeId is null cannot be joined to anything, one whose snmpInterface is a string instead of the inlined object, an ipAddress of \"192.0.2..300\" that is not an address, an all-zero physAddr, a physAddr that is not hex at all, and an snmpInterface whose ifType is the string \"24\" rather than the number - softwareLoopback either way. Proves that the only record dropped is the one with no id, that it is logged and counted, that every other record survives with the unreadable field simply absent, and that a value which is not an address never reaches an interface. Nothing here may abort the run: a script that subscripts a string or hands a None to network_interface takes the whole collection down with it, not just the row.",
